NNAAMMEE
       delv - DNS lookup and validation utility

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ddeellvv [@server] [ [--44] | [--66] ] [--aa anchor-file] [--bb address] [--cc class]
       [--dd  level]  [--ii]  [--mm] [--pp port#] [--qq name] [--tt type] [--xx addr] [name]
       [type] [class] [queryopt...]

       ddeellvv [--hh]

       ddeellvv [--vv]

       ddeellvv [queryopt...] [query...]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       ddeellvv is a tool for sending DNS queries and validating the results,  us‐
       ing the same internal resolver and validator logic as _n_a_m_e_d.

       ddeellvv  sends  to a specified name server all queries needed to fetch and
       validate the requested  data;  this  includes  the  original  requested
       query,  subsequent queries to follow CNAME or DNAME chains, queries for
       DNSKEY, and DS records to establish a chain of trust for DNSSEC valida‐
       tion. It does not perform iterative resolution, but simulates  the  be‐
       havior  of  a name server configured for DNSSEC validating and forward‐
       ing.

       By default, responses are validated using the built-in DNSSEC trust an‐
       chor for the root zone ("."). Records returned by ddeellvv are either fully
       validated or were not signed. If validation fails,  an  explanation  of
       the  failure  is  included in the output; the validation process can be
       traced in detail. Because ddeellvv does not rely on an external  server  to
       carry  out  validation, it can be used to check the validity of DNS re‐
       sponses in environments where local name servers may not  be  trustwor‐
       thy.

       Unless  it  is told to query a specific name server, ddeellvv tries each of
       the servers listed in //eettcc//rreessoollvv..ccoonnff. If no usable  server  addresses
       are found, ddeellvv sends queries to the localhost addresses (127.0.0.1 for
       IPv4, ::1 for IPv6).

       When  no  command-line arguments or options are given, ddeellvv performs an
       NS query for "." (the root zone).

SSIIMMPPLLEE UUSSAAGGEE
       A typical invocation of ddeellvv looks like:

          delv @server name type

       where:

       sseerrvveerr is the name or IP address of the name server to query. This  can
              be an IPv4 address in dotted-decimal notation or an IPv6 address
              in  colon-delimited  notation. When the supplied sseerrvveerr argument
              is a hostname, ddeellvv resolves that name before querying that name
              server (note, however, that this initial lookup is _n_o_t validated
              by DNSSEC).

              If no  sseerrvveerr  argument  is  provided,  ddeellvv  consults  //eettcc//rree‐‐
              ssoollvv..ccoonnff;  if  an  address  is found there, it queries the name
              server at that address. If either of the _-_4 or _-_6 options is  in
              use,  then  only  addresses  for the corresponding transport are
              tried. If no usable addresses are found, ddeellvv sends  queries  to
              the localhost addresses (127.0.0.1 for IPv4, ::1 for IPv6).

       nnaammee   is the domain name to be looked up.

       ttyyppee   indicates  what  type  of  query  is required - ANY, A, MX, etc.
              ttyyppee can be any valid query type. If no ttyyppee  argument  is  sup‐
              plied, ddeellvv performs a lookup for an A record.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --aa aanncchhoorr--ffiillee
              This option specifies a file from which to read DNSSEC trust an‐
              chors.  The  default  is ////eettcc//bbiinndd..kkeeyyss, which is included with
              BIND 9 and contains one or more trust anchors for the root  zone
              (".").

              Keys that do not match the root zone name are ignored. An alter‐
              nate key name can be specified using the _+_r_o_o_t option.

              Note:  When reading the trust anchor file, ddeellvv treats ttrruusstt--aann‐‐
              cchhoorrss, iinniittiiaall--kkeeyy, and ssttaattiicc--kkeeyy identically. That is,  for  a
              managed key, it is the _i_n_i_t_i_a_l key that is trusted; _R_F_C _5_0_1_1 key
              management  is  not  supported.  ddeellvv  does not consult the man‐
              aged-keys database maintained by _n_a_m_e_d, which means that if  ei‐
              ther  of the keys in ////eettcc//bbiinndd..kkeeyyss is revoked and rolled over,
              ////eettcc//bbiinndd..kkeeyyss must be updated  to  use  DNSSEC  validation  in
              ddeellvv.

       --bb aaddddrreessss
              This  option sets the source IP address of the query to aaddddrreessss.
              This must be a valid address on one of the host's network inter‐
              faces, or 00..00..00..00, or ::::. An optional source port may be  speci‐
              fied by appending ##<<ppoorrtt>>

       --cc ccllaassss
              This  option  sets  the query class for the requested data. Cur‐
              rently, only class "IN" is supported in ddeellvv and any other value
              is ignored.

       --dd lleevveell
              This option sets the systemwide debug level to  lleevveell.  The  al‐
              lowed  range  is  from 0 to 99. The default is 0 (no debugging).
              Debugging traces from ddeellvv become  more  verbose  as  the  debug
              level  increases.  See the _+_m_t_r_a_c_e, _+_r_t_r_a_c_e, and _+_v_t_r_a_c_e options
              below for additional debugging details.

       --hh     This option displays the ddeellvv help usage output and exits.

       --ii     This option sets insecure mode, which disables  internal  DNSSEC
              validation. (Note, however, that this does not set the CD bit on
              upstream  queries.  If  the  server  being queried is performing
              DNSSEC validation, then it does not return  invalid  data;  this
              can  cause ddeellvv to time out. When it is necessary to examine in‐
              valid data to debug a DNSSEC problem, use _d_i_g _+_c_d.)

       --mm     This option enables memory usage debugging.

       --pp ppoorrtt##
              This option specifies a destination port to use for queries, in‐
              stead of the standard DNS port number 53. This  option  is  used
              with  a  name  server  that  has  been  configured to listen for
              queries on a non-standard port number.

       --qq nnaammee
              This option sets the query name to nnaammee. While  the  query  name
              can  be  specified  without using the _-_q option, it is sometimes
              necessary to disambiguate names from types or classes (for exam‐
              ple, when looking up the name "ns",  which  could  be  misinter‐
              preted as the type NS, or "ch", which could be misinterpreted as
              class CH).

       --tt ttyyppee
              This  option sets the query type to ttyyppee, which can be any valid
              query type supported in BIND 9 except for  zone  transfer  types
              AXFR  and  IXFR.  As  with  _-_q,  this  is  useful to distinguish
              query-name types or classes when they are ambiguous. It is some‐
              times necessary to disambiguate names from types.

              The default query type is "A", unless the _-_x option is  supplied
              to indicate a reverse lookup, in which case it is "PTR".

       --vv     This option prints the ddeellvv version and exits.

       --xx aaddddrr
              This  option  performs a reverse lookup, mapping an address to a
              name. aaddddrr is an IPv4 address in dotted-decimal notation,  or  a
              colon-delimited  IPv6 address. When _-_x is used, there is no need
              to provide the nnaammee or ttyyppee arguments; ddeellvv  automatically  per‐
              forms a lookup for a name like 1111..1122..1133..1100..iinn--aaddddrr..aarrppaa and sets
              the query type to PTR. IPv6 addresses are looked up using nibble
              format under the IP6.ARPA domain.

       --44     This option forces ddeellvv to only use IPv4.

       --66     This option forces ddeellvv to only use IPv6.

QQUUEERRYY OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       ddeellvv  provides  a  number of query options which affect the way results
       are displayed, and in some cases the way lookups are performed.

       Each query option is identified by a keyword preceded by  a  plus  sign
       (++). Some keywords set or reset an option. These may be preceded by the
       string  nnoo to negate the meaning of that keyword. Other keywords assign
       values to options like the timeout interval. They have the  form  ++kkeeyy‐‐
       wwoorrdd==vvaalluuee. The query options are:

       ++ccddffllaagg,, ++nnooccddffllaagg
              This  option  controls whether to set the CD (checking disabled)
              bit in queries sent by ddeellvv.  This  may  be  useful  when  trou‐
              bleshooting DNSSEC problems from behind a validating resolver. A
              validating  resolver  blocks invalid responses, making it diffi‐
              cult to retrieve them for  analysis.  Setting  the  CD  flag  on
              queries  causes  the resolver to return invalid responses, which
              ddeellvv can then validate internally and report the errors  in  de‐
              tail.

       ++ccllaassss,, ++nnooccllaassss
              This  option controls whether to display the CLASS when printing
              a record. The default is to display the CLASS.

       ++ttttll,, ++nnoottttll
              This option controls whether to display the TTL when printing  a
              record. The default is to display the TTL.

       ++rrttrraaccee,, ++nnoorrttrraaccee
              This  option  toggles  resolver  fetch logging. This reports the
              name and type of each query sent by ddeellvv in the process of  car‐
              rying  out  the resolution and validation process, including the
              original query and all subsequent queries to follow  CNAMEs  and
              to establish a chain of trust for DNSSEC validation.

              This  is  equivalent to setting the debug level to 1 in the "re‐
              solver" logging category. Setting the systemwide debug level  to
              1  using  the  _-_d  option  produces the same output, but affects
              other logging categories as well.

       ++mmttrraaccee,, ++nnoommttrraaccee
              This option toggles message logging. This  produces  a  detailed
              dump  of the responses received by ddeellvv in the process of carry‐
              ing out the resolution and validation process.

              This is equivalent to setting the debug  level  to  10  for  the
              "packets" module of the "resolver" logging category. Setting the
              systemwide  debug  level  to 10 using the _-_d option produces the
              same output, but affects other logging categories as well.

       ++vvttrraaccee,, ++nnoovvttrraaccee
              This option toggles validation logging. This shows the  internal
              process  of  the validator as it determines whether an answer is
              validly signed, unsigned, or invalid.

              This is equivalent to setting the debug level to 3 for the "val‐
              idator" module of the "dnssec"  logging  category.  Setting  the
              systemwide  debug  level  to  3 using the _-_d option produces the
              same output, but affects other logging categories as well.

       ++sshhoorrtt,, ++nnoosshhoorrtt
              This option toggles between verbose and terse answers.  The  de‐
              fault is to print the answer in a verbose form.

       ++ccoommmmeennttss,, ++nnooccoommmmeennttss
              This  option toggles the display of comment lines in the output.
              The default is to print comments.

       ++rrrrccoommmmeennttss,, ++nnoorrrrccoommmmeennttss
              This option toggles the display of per-record  comments  in  the
              output (for example, human-readable key information about DNSKEY
              records). The default is to print per-record comments.

       ++ccrryyppttoo,, ++nnooccrryyppttoo
              This  option  toggles  the  display  of  cryptographic fields in
              DNSSEC records. The contents of these fields are unnecessary  to
              debug most DNSSEC validation failures and removing them makes it
              easier to see the common failures. The default is to display the
              fields.  When omitted, they are replaced by the string [[oommiitttteedd]]
              or, in the DNSKEY case, the key ID is displayed as the  replace‐
              ment, e.g. [[ kkeeyy iidd == vvaalluuee ]].

       ++ttrruusstt,, ++nnoottrruusstt
              This  option  controls  whether  to display the trust level when
              printing a record.  The default is to display the trust level.

       ++sspplliitt[[==WW]],, ++nnoosspplliitt
              This option splits long hex- or base64-formatted fields  in  re‐
              source  records  into chunks of WW characters (where WW is rounded
              up to the nearest multiple of 4). ++nnoosspplliitt  or  ++sspplliitt==00  causes
              fields  not to be split at all. The default is 56 characters, or
              44 characters when multiline mode is active.

       ++aallll,, ++nnooaallll
              This option  sets  or  clears  the  display  options  _+_c_o_m_m_e_n_t_s,
              _+_r_r_c_o_m_m_e_n_t_s, and _+_t_r_u_s_t as a group.

       ++mmuullttiilliinnee,, ++nnoommuullttiilliinnee
              This  option prints long records (such as RRSIG, DNSKEY, and SOA
              records) in a verbose multi-line format with human-readable com‐
              ments. The default is to print each record on a single line,  to
              facilitate machine parsing of the ddeellvv output.

       ++ddnnsssseecc,, ++nnooddnnsssseecc
              This  option  indicates  whether to display RRSIG records in the
              ddeellvv output.  The default is to do so. Note that (unlike in _d_i_g)
              this does _n_o_t control whether to request DNSSEC  records  or  to
              validate  them. DNSSEC records are always requested, and valida‐
              tion always occurs  unless  suppressed  by  the  use  of  _-_i  or
              _+_n_o_r_o_o_t.

       ++rroooott[[==RROOOOTT]],, ++nnoorroooott
              This  option  indicates  whether  to perform conventional DNSSEC
              validation, and if so, specifies the name of a trust anchor. The
              default is to validate using a trust anchor  of  "."  (the  root
              zone),  for  which there is a built-in key. If specifying a dif‐
              ferent trust anchor, then _-_a must be used to specify a file con‐
              taining the key.

       ++ttccpp,, ++nnoottccpp
              This option controls whether to use TCP  when  sending  queries.
              The  default  is to use UDP unless a truncated response has been
              received.

       ++uunnkknnoowwnnffoorrmmaatt,, ++nnoouunnkknnoowwnnffoorrmmaatt
              This option prints all RDATA  in  unknown  RR-type  presentation
              format  (_R_F_C  _3_5_9_7).   The  default  is to print RDATA for known
              types in the type's presentation format.

       ++yyaammll,, ++nnooyyaammll
              This option prints response data in YAML format.

